/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*------------------------------ File ADS.dox ------------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_ADS */
/** \defgroup theory_ADS Theory of the foundations
 \ingroup area_ADS

 \details The headers of `include/ADS` itself hold what the other areas take
 for granted: the abstract data types of chapters 3 and 4 of the book as
 concepts of C++20 (`ADT.h`), the preconditions, the postconditions and the
 invariants that the operations check at run time (`Contract.h`), the
 iterators that the containers share (`Iterators.h`), and the primitives of
 the processor in one portable form (`Portable.h`). This page collects the
 definitions behind them, i.e., what a specification of an abstract data type
 is and what its axioms say (see \ref theory_ADS_notation and
 \ref theory_ADS_adt), what a contract is and why each kind of contract is
 checked at its own level (see \ref theory_ADS_contracts), and the categories
 of the iterators with the cost of their steps (see
 \ref theory_ADS_iterators), while the arithmetic of the portable primitives
 (see \ref theory_ADS_portable) and the typed modifications by which a
 structure tells its observers what changed (see
 \ref theory_ADS_modifications) and the capabilities that an algorithm may ask
 of a structure (see \ref theory_ADS_capabilities), and the algebra by which
 an aggregate chooses its update rule (see \ref theory_ADS_algebra) close the
 page. The algorithms of the iterators have their pseudocode on the page of
 `Iterators.h`, in the comment of their function.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_ADS_notation Definitions and notation

 A <em>specification</em> of an abstract data type is a triple
 \f$ ( D , F , A ) \f$ (section 3.2 of the book): \f$ D \f$ is a set of
 domains, one of which is the <em>designated</em> domain, i.e., the type being
 defined (e.g., the stacks), and the others are the auxiliary ones (e.g., the
 items and the booleans); \f$ F \f$ is a set of operations, each a function
 among the domains of \f$ D \f$; \f$ A \f$ is a set of axioms, which give the
 semantics of \f$ F \f$. The pair \f$ ( D , F ) \f$ is the <em>syntactic</em>
 specification, which a concept of C++20 states as the names of the
 operations and the types of their arguments and results, and \f$ A \f$ is the
 <em>semantic</em> one, which no compiler checks. The semantics may be given
 in two ways: (i) <em>algebraic</em>, by equations among the operations, those
 that observe or remove written on those that construct, as in
 \f$ \mathrm{top}( \mathrm{push}( S , v ) ) = v \f$; (ii)
 <em>constructive</em>, by the precondition and the postcondition of each
 operation, written in terms of a type already known (e.g., the finite
 sequences of items). The library states (i) in the comments of `ADT.h` and
 checks (ii) in the code, by the macros of `Contract.h`, and its tests check
 both on every realization.

 The operations are of four kinds: the <em>constructors</em>, which make a
 value of the type (`create()`, `insert()`, `push()`); the
 <em>modifiers</em>, which change it; the <em>observers</em>, which return a
 property of it (`empty()`, `top()`, `read()`); the <em>iterators</em>, which
 visit its items. A realization meets the requirement of
 <em>abstraction</em>, i.e., a program that uses the type does not depend on
 how it is realized, and that of <em>protection</em>, i.e., only the
 operations of the type act on its values; hence each realization is a class
 whose data are private, and one realization can replace another under the
 same concept (Liskov and Zilles, Parnas).

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ ( D , F , A ) \f$</td>
     <td>a specification: the domains, the operations and the
         axioms</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ s_0 , \ldots , s_{n-1} \f$</td>
     <td>the items of a SEQUENCE of length \f$ n \f$, \f$ s_i \f$ the item
         of index \f$ i \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{pos}_i \f$</td>
     <td>the position of \f$ s_i \f$, of the type <tt>Pos</tt> of the
         realization, for \f$ 0 \le i < n \f$; \f$ \mathit{pos}_{-1} \f$ and
         \f$ \mathit{pos}_n \f$ are the positions before the first item and
         after the last one, on which <tt>finished()</tt> is true</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \{ P \}\ \mathit{op}\ \{ Q \} \f$</td>
     <td>a contract: if the precondition \f$ P \f$ holds when the operation
         \f$ \mathit{op} \f$ starts, the postcondition \f$ Q \f$ holds when it
         ends</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ I \f$</td>
     <td>the invariant of a structure, a property of its representation that
         holds between any two of its operations</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ h \f$</td>
     <td>the height of a binary tree, the length of its longest path from
         the root</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ c \f$, \f$ m \f$</td>
     <td>the capacity of a circular vector, and the number of places of a
         table</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_ADS_adt Abstract data types and their axioms

 The concepts of `ADT.h` are the five abstract data types of chapters 3 and 4
 of the book, and the headers of the areas realize them, each in several
 ways; the concepts state the syntactic specification, so that a function
 template written on `Stack` takes any of the stacks of `include/ADS/stack`,
 and the axioms below are their semantics.

 <table>
 <caption>The concepts of <tt>ADT.h</tt>, their operations and the areas that
          realize them</caption>
 <tr><th>Concept</th><th>Abstract type</th><th>Operations</th>
     <th>Section of the book</th><th>Area</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Sequence</tt></td><td>SEQUENCE</td>
     <td><tt>empty()</tt>, <tt>finished()</tt>, <tt>head()</tt>,
         <tt>tail()</tt>, <tt>next()</tt>, <tt>prev()</tt>,
         <tt>insert()</tt>, <tt>remove()</tt>, <tt>read()</tt>,
         <tt>write()</tt></td><td>3.2.1</td>
     <td><tt>include/ADS/sequence</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Set</tt></td><td>SET</td>
     <td><tt>size()</tt>, <tt>contains()</tt>, <tt>insert()</tt>,
         <tt>remove()</tt></td><td>3.2.2</td>
     <td><tt>include/ADS/set</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Dictionary</tt></td><td>DICTIONARY</td>
     <td><tt>lookup()</tt>, <tt>insert()</tt>, <tt>remove()</tt></td>
     <td>3.2.3</td><td><tt>include/ADS/dictionary</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Stack</tt></td><td>STACK</td>
     <td><tt>empty()</tt>, <tt>push()</tt>, <tt>pop()</tt>,
         <tt>top()</tt></td><td>4.3.1</td>
     <td><tt>include/ADS/stack</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Queue</tt></td><td>QUEUE</td>
     <td><tt>empty()</tt>, <tt>enqueue()</tt>, <tt>dequeue()</tt>,
         <tt>top()</tt></td><td>4.3.1</td>
     <td><tt>include/ADS/queue</tt></td></tr>
 </table>

 The axioms of the STACK, with \f$ \mathrm{create} \f$ the empty stack, are
 \f[
   \begin{array}{ll}
   \mathrm{empty}( \mathrm{create} ) = \mathbf{true} , &
   \mathrm{empty}( \mathrm{push}( S , v ) ) = \mathbf{false} , \cr
   \mathrm{top}( \mathrm{push}( S , v ) ) = v , &
   \mathrm{pop}( \mathrm{push}( S , v ) ) = S ,
   \end{array} \tag{1}
 \f]
 while \f$ \mathrm{top}( \mathrm{create} ) \f$ and
 \f$ \mathrm{pop}( \mathrm{create} ) \f$ are undefined, i.e., `top()` and
 `pop()` are partial, with the precondition that the stack is not empty. Those
 of the QUEUE, for \f$ Q \f$ not empty, are
 \f[
   \mathrm{top}( \mathrm{enqueue}( Q , v ) ) = \mathrm{top}( Q ) , \qquad
   \mathrm{dequeue}( \mathrm{enqueue}( Q , v ) ) =
   \mathrm{enqueue}( \mathrm{dequeue}( Q ) , v ) , \tag{2}
 \f]
 with \f$ \mathrm{empty}( \mathrm{create} ) = \mathbf{true} \f$,
 \f$ \mathrm{empty}( \mathrm{enqueue}( Q , v ) ) = \mathbf{false} \f$ for
 any \f$ Q \f$, \f$ \mathrm{top}( \mathrm{enqueue}( \mathrm{create} , v ) )
 = v \f$ and \f$ \mathrm{dequeue}( \mathrm{enqueue}( \mathrm{create} , v ) )
 = \mathrm{create} \f$; the difference between (1) and (2) is the whole
 difference between the two types, since their syntactic specifications are
 the same up to the names. Those of the SET and of the DICTIONARY define the
 observer on each constructor, e.g., for the SET
 \f[
   \mathrm{contains}( x , \mathrm{insert}( y , A ) ) =
   ( x = y ) \lor \mathrm{contains}( x , A ) , \qquad
   \mathrm{contains}( x , \mathrm{remove}( y , A ) ) =
   ( x \ne y ) \land \mathrm{contains}( x , A ) , \tag{3}
 \f]
 with \f$ \mathrm{contains}( x , \mathrm{create} ) = \mathbf{false} \f$, so
 that inserting an item already there, or removing one that is not, leaves
 the set as it is (the library relaxes in this way the preconditions
 \f$ x \notin A \f$ and \f$ x \in A \f$ that some specifications state).

 <em>Lemma 1 (the normal form of the stacks).</em> By (1), every stack built
 by \f$ \mathrm{create} \f$, \f$ \mathrm{push} \f$ and \f$ \mathrm{pop} \f$,
 each \f$ \mathrm{pop} \f$ applied to a stack not empty, is equal to a term
 \f[
   \mathrm{push}( \cdots \mathrm{push}( \mathrm{push}( \mathrm{create} ,
   v_1 ) , v_2 ) \cdots , v_k ) , \quad k \ge 0 , \tag{4}
 \f]
 and the observers of a term (4) are known: it is empty if and only if
 \f$ k = 0 \f$, and its top is \f$ v_k \f$.

 Indeed, by induction on the number of operations of the term, a
 \f$ \mathrm{push} \f$ applied to a term (4) is a term (4), and a
 \f$ \mathrm{pop} \f$ applied to a term (4) with \f$ k \ge 1 \f$ is, by the
 last axiom of (1), the term (4) of \f$ v_1 , \ldots , v_{k-1} \f$. The axioms
 are therefore <em>sufficiently complete</em>: they decide the value of every
 observer on every stack, which is what makes them a specification rather
 than a list of properties (Guttag and Horning). The same holds for the queues
 by (2), the normal form being made of \f$ \mathrm{enqueue} \f$ alone (a
 \f$ \mathrm{dequeue} \f$ of a normal form of \f$ k \ge 1 \f$ items is, by
 induction on \f$ k \f$, the normal form of its last \f$ k - 1 \f$ items).
 For the sets, (3) decides \f$ \mathrm{contains}( x , A ) \f$ for every
 \f$ x \f$ and every term \f$ A \f$, by induction on \f$ A \f$; hence, if
 two sets are taken as equal when they contain the same items, every term is
 equal to a term made of \f$ \mathrm{insert} \f$ alone, on the distinct
 items that it contains, in any order.

 The SEQUENCE is specified in the constructive way, by its positions. As in
 the book, \f$ \mathrm{insert}( p , v ) \f$ with \f$ p = \mathit{pos}_i \f$
 puts \f$ v \f$ at the index \f$ i \f$, the old items from the index \f$ i \f$
 moving one place on, and returns the position of the new item, while
 \f$ \mathrm{remove}( p ) \f$ with \f$ p = \mathit{pos}_i \f$ removes the item
 of index \f$ i \f$ and returns the position of the new item of index
 \f$ i \f$; hence the axioms
 \f[
   \mathrm{read}( \mathrm{insert}( p , v ) ) = v , \qquad
   \mathrm{remove}( \mathrm{insert}( p , v ) ) = s , \qquad
   \mathrm{read}( \mathrm{write}( p , v ) ) = v , \tag{5}
 \f]
 the last with the other items unchanged, and
 \f$ \mathrm{empty}( s ) = \mathrm{finished}( \mathrm{head}( s ) ) \f$, which
 makes `empty()` redundant. What a position is depends on the realization
 (an index, the address of a cell, a cursor, see \ref theory_sequence), and so
 does the cost of each operation, while (5) holds on all of them.

 \section theory_ADS_contracts Contracts

 A <em>contract</em> of an operation is a pair of a precondition \f$ P \f$
 and a postcondition \f$ Q \f$, written \f$ \{ P \}\ \mathit{op}\ \{ Q \} \f$
 after Hoare: the caller ensures \f$ P \f$, and the operation, under
 \f$ P \f$, ensures \f$ Q \f$; a structure has also an <em>invariant</em>
 \f$ I \f$, which each operation that changes it may assume at its start and
 must restore at its end, i.e., \f$ \{ P \land I \}\ \mathit{op}\ \{ Q \land I
 \} \f$. A violated precondition is a bug of the caller, a violated
 postcondition or invariant a bug of the operation (Meyer); in either case
 `Contract.h` throws `contract_violation`, a `std::logic_error` whose message
 names the kind of contract, the condition, the function and the place in the
 source.

 Checking a contract costs time, and the costs differ by orders of magnitude:
 \f$ \mathrm{empty}() \f$ before a \f$ \mathrm{pop}() \f$ costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$,
 the order of a heap after an insertion costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, and that a
 vector is sorted before a binary search costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ against
 the \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ of the search. A check of cost \f$ g( n ) \f$ added
 to an operation of cost \f$ t( n ) \f$ makes it cost
 \f$ O( t( n ) + g( n ) ) \f$, which is the same bound only if
 \f$ g( n ) = O( t( n ) ) \f$; an invariant of \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ checked
 after each of \f$ k \f$ operations of \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ makes them cost
 \f$ \Theta( k n ) \f$, i.e., a linear algorithm quadratic. The library
 therefore sorts its checks by their cost with respect to the operation, and
 the level `ADS_CONTRACTS` chooses which are evaluated.

 <table>
 <caption>The contracts of <tt>Contract.h</tt> and the level from which each is
          checked</caption>
 <tr><th>Macro</th><th>Contract</th><th>Its cost</th><th>Level</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ADS_PRE</tt></td><td>a precondition</td>
     <td>at most that of the operation</td><td>1 (the default, 0 with
     <tt>NDEBUG</tt>)</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ADS_POST</tt></td><td>a postcondition</td>
     <td>at most that of the operation</td><td>2</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ADS_INVARIANT</tt></td><td>the invariant of a structure</td>
     <td>usually \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td><td>3</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ADS_AUDIT</tt></td><td>a precondition or a postcondition</td>
     <td>more than the operation</td><td>3</td></tr>
 </table>

 At the level 1 the bounds of the operations hold as stated, since every
 precondition checked costs no more than its operation; at the level 3 they
 do not, and the level is meant for the tests. A condition is compiled at
 every level, so that it stays correct C++, but it is evaluated only from its
 own level; hence it must have no side effects, since a program must behave
 in the same way at every level. The macros mirror the `pre`, `post` and
 `contract_assert` of the contracts of C++26, by which they can be replaced
 when the compilers have them.

 \section theory_ADS_iterators Iterators

 An <em>iterator</em> is a position in a container that can be read and moved
 to the next one, and the standard library sorts the iterators into
 <em>categories</em> by the moves they allow in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time; an
 algorithm states the category it needs (`std::sort()` a random-access
 iterator, `std::reverse()` a bidirectional one), and the iterators of
 `Iterators.h` give the containers of the library the category that their
 representation allows, so that the range-for, the algorithms of `std` and
 of `std::ranges`, and those of Boost visit them.

 <table>
 <caption>The iterators of <tt>Iterators.h</tt> and their categories</caption>
 <tr><th>Iterator</th><th>Walks</th><th>Category</th>
     <th>Cost of a step</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>LinkedIterator</tt></td><td>cells linked by pointers</td>
     <td>forward</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>IndexIterator</tt></td><td>cells of a vector linked by
     indices</td><td>forward</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CircularIterator</tt></td><td>a circular vector</td>
     <td>random access</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>RankIterator</tt></td><td>a container read by rank,
     <tt>c[ i ]</tt></td><td>random access</td><td>that of
     <tt>c[ i ]</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>IndirectIterator</tt></td><td>a vector of pointers, read
     through them</td><td>random access</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SlotIterator</tt></td><td>the occupied places of a table</td>
     <td>bidirectional</td><td>\f$ O( m / n ) \f$ amortized over a whole
     visit</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>KeyIterator</tt></td><td>the keys of what another iterator
     reads</td><td>that of the other, up to bidirectional</td>
     <td>that of the other</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>PathIterator</tt></td><td>a binary tree in symmetric order, by
     the path from the root</td><td>bidirectional</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized over a whole visit, \f$ O( h ) \f$ at
     most</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>WalkIterator</tt></td><td>the nodes of a tree by a policy</td>
     <td>forward</td><td>that of the policy</td></tr>
 </table>

 A circular vector of capacity \f$ c \f$ whose first item is at the place
 \f$ \mathit{head} \f$ holds its item of index \f$ i \f$, counted from the
 head, at the place
 \f[
   ( \mathit{head} + i ) \bmod c , \quad 0 \le i < n \le c , \tag{6}
 \f]
 so that `CircularIterator` keeps the index \f$ i \f$ rather than the place,
 its moves and its differences are those of the integers, and the order of
 the items is that of the queue rather than that of the places.

 A table of \f$ m \f$ places of which \f$ n \f$ are occupied is visited by
 `SlotIterator` in \f$ \Theta( m ) \f$ time, since each place is examined
 once, i.e., in \f$ O( m / n ) \f$ amortized time per item, which is
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ when the load \f$ n / m \f$ is bounded from below by a
 constant, as in the hash tables that halve their table when they get too
 empty; a single step may examine
 \f$ \Theta( m ) \f$ places.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the visit in symmetric order by the path).</em> Let
 `PathIterator` keep the path \f$ \pi \f$ from the root to the current node
 \f$ x \f$. The next node in symmetric order is the leftmost node of the right
 subtree of \f$ x \f$ if \f$ x \f$ has a right child, and otherwise the lowest
 ancestor of \f$ x \f$ whose left subtree contains \f$ x \f$; a whole visit of
 a tree of \f$ n \f$ nodes costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, i.e., \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
 amortized per step, and a single step costs \f$ O( h ) \f$.

 Indeed, the nodes of the right subtree of \f$ x \f$ come right after
 \f$ x \f$, the leftmost first; if there is no right subtree, all the nodes
 below the ancestor \f$ y \f$ described come before \f$ y \f$ and \f$ x \f$
 is the last of its left subtree, so that \f$ y \f$ follows \f$ x \f$. The
 path \f$ \pi \f$ gives the ancestors without links to the parents, and a step
 that goes down pushes the nodes it meets on \f$ \pi \f$, a step that goes up
 pops them. Each node is pushed once, when the visit first reaches it from its
 parent, and popped once, when the visit leaves its subtree; hence a whole
 visit makes \f$ n \f$ pushes and \f$ n \f$ pops, and since a step moves
 along one path from the root, it makes at most \f$ h \f$ of them. The path
 takes \f$ O( h ) \f$ space, against the link to the parent in every node
 that an iterator with no path needs, i.e., \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ space; on a
 balanced tree \f$ h = O( \log n ) \f$.

 \section theory_ADS_portable Portable primitives

 The product of two words of 64 bits is a number of 128 bits, which the
 processors give in one instruction and the languages do not; `Portable.h`
 takes it from the builtin of the compiler where there is one, and otherwise
 computes it on the halves of 32 bits of the factors. With
 \f$ a = a_1 2^{32} + a_0 \f$ and \f$ b = b_1 2^{32} + b_0 \f$,
 \f$ 0 \le a_0 , a_1 , b_0 , b_1 < 2^{32} \f$, one has
 \f[
   a b = a_1 b_1 2^{64} + ( a_0 b_1 + a_1 b_0 ) 2^{32} + a_0 b_0 , \tag{7}
 \f]
 four products of 32 bits, each of which fits in 64 bits. The sum of the
 middle terms with the high half of \f$ a_0 b_0 \f$ is computed on the low
 halves of the middle products, i.e., as three numbers below \f$ 2^{32} \f$,
 whose sum is below \f$ 3 \cdot 2^{32} \f$ and fits in a word with no
 overflow; its high half is the carry into the high word of the product.
 This computation is also `wideProductHalves()`, which the integers of any
 length of `BigInteger.h` call in place of the builtin when
 `ADS_BIGINT_PORTABLE` is defined, so that the library has one product of two
 words. In the same way, the signed integers of 128 bits, where the compiler
 has no `__int128`, are two words in two's complement, and the sum carries 1
 into the high word exactly when the sum of the low words, modulo
 \f$ 2^{64} \f$, is smaller than either of them. These are details of the
 implementation, out of the API reference.

 \section theory_ADS_modifications Typed modifications

 A structure whose items have stable handles is, for the observers, a
 partial map \f$ \sigma \f$ from the handles to the states of the items,
 where the state of an item is the tuple of its attributes (its key, its
 value, and any other attribute \f$ A \f$, e.g., the cost of an arc), and
 \f$ \sigma( h ) = \bot \f$ when no item has the handle \f$ h \f$. An event of
 an item is a step of \f$ \sigma \f$ at one handle that carries both its
 ends: \f$ \mathrm{Inserted}( h , s ) \f$ takes \f$ \sigma( h ) \f$ from
 \f$ \bot \f$ to \f$ s \f$, \f$ \mathrm{Erased}( h , s ) \f$ from \f$ s \f$ to
 \f$ \bot \f$, and \f$ \mathrm{Changed}_A( h , a , b ) \f$ changes the
 attribute \f$ A \f$ of \f$ \sigma( h ) \f$ from \f$ a \f$ to \f$ b \f$; the
 events of the whole structure (\f$ \mathrm{Cleared} \f$, which makes
 \f$ \sigma \f$ empty, \f$ \mathrm{Rebuilt} \f$, after which any \f$ \sigma \f$
 is possible, \f$ \mathrm{Shifted}_A( \delta ) \f$, which adds \f$ \delta \f$
 to the attribute \f$ A \f$ of every item, \f$ \mathrm{Replaced}_A \f$ and
 the relocations) are not of one item. A sequence of events is a
 <em>history</em> of \f$ \sigma_0 \f$ when each event starts from the state
 that the previous ones leave. Since an event carries the old state as well
 as the new one, an observer that reads the events only, and not the
 structure, is correct whatever happened after them, and every event of an
 item has an inverse, the step back, which `inverse()` gives.

 The <em>net effect</em> of a batch \f$ B \f$ is a sequence \f$ C \f$ that
 leads from \f$ \sigma_0 \f$ to the state to which \f$ B \f$ leads. The
 coalescing of `Modification.h` builds it in three steps: (i) everything
 before the last \f$ \mathrm{Cleared} \f$ or \f$ \mathrm{Rebuilt} \f$ is
 dropped, since the state after them does not depend on it; (ii) the events
 that are not of one item split what remains into runs, and they are kept in
 place, consecutive shifts of one attribute added into one (dropped if the
 sum is zero) and consecutive replacements of one attribute made one; (iii)
 within a run the events of each handle are reduced, in the order of their
 first event, by pushing them one by one on a list \f$ N \f$ of net events of
 the handle with the rules
 \f[
 \begin{array}{rcl}
 N \cdot \mathrm{Inserted}( h , s ) \cdot \mathrm{Erased}( h , s ) & \to &
   N , \cr
 \mathrm{Inserted}( h , s ) \cdot \mathrm{Changed}_A( h , a , b ) & \to &
   \mathrm{Inserted}( h , s[ A := b ] ) \quad A \in \{ \mathrm{Key} ,
   \mathrm{Value} \} , \cr
 \mathrm{Changed}_A( h , a , b ) \cdot \mathrm{Changed}_A( h , b , c ) &
   \to & \mathrm{Changed}_A( h , a , c ) , \ \text{or nothing if } a = c ,
   \cr
 \mathrm{Changed}_A( h , a , b ) \cdot \mathrm{Erased}( h , s ) & \to &
   \mathrm{Erased}( h , s[ A := a ] ) , \cr
 \mathrm{Erased}( h , s ) \cdot \mathrm{Inserted}( h , t ) & \to &
   \prod_{A : s_A \ne t_A} \mathrm{Changed}_A( h , s_A , t_A ) ,
 \end{array} \tag{8}
 \f]
 where a change of an attribute moves past the changes of the other
 attributes of the handle (they commute, since they write disjoint parts of
 the state), a change with \f$ a = b \f$ (a touch) is dropped, and the last
 rule applies only when the structure emits the changes that it needs (a
 structure with no \f$ \mathrm{KeyChanged} \f$ keeps the erasure and the
 insertion of an item whose key differs). In the third rule
 \f$ c \f$ is the value of the reduction of \f$ A \f$: the last value by
 default, the least or the largest of \f$ b \f$ and \f$ c \f$ for an
 attribute whose changes are merged by a minimum or a maximum (the absolute
 values of a relaxation, which need not be a history), and
 \f$ b + ( c - b' ) \f$ for one whose changes add up, \f$ b' \f$ being the
 old value of the second change.

 <b>Lemma 1.</b> <em>Let \f$ B \f$ be a history of \f$ \sigma_0 \f$ whose
 attributes have the reduction `last`, and \f$ C \f$ its net effect by (i),
 (ii) and (iii). Then \f$ C \f$ is a history of \f$ \sigma_0 \f$ that leads
 to the state to which \f$ B \f$ leads, \f$ |C| \le |B| \f$, and \f$ C \f$ is
 built in \f$ O( |B| ) \f$ expected time when the number of kinds of event is
 a constant.</em>

 <em>Proof.</em> Step (i) is correct because \f$ \mathrm{Cleared} \f$ and
 \f$ \mathrm{Rebuilt} \f$ start from any state. The events of different
 handles within a run commute, since each reads and writes the state of its
 own handle only; hence a run can be replayed handle by handle, and it
 suffices that each rule of (8) replaces two steps at one handle by steps
 that lead from the same state to the same state. The first rule takes
 \f$ \bot \f$ to \f$ s \f$ and back; the second inserts directly the state
 after the change; the third composes two changes of one attribute, and
 vanishes when the attribute returns to its value; the fourth erases from
 the state before the change, which is the state that the erasure of the
 history takes away, the attribute restored; the fifth goes from \f$ s \f$ to
 \f$ t \f$ by one change per attribute that differs. Each rule leaves at most
 as many events as it takes, so that \f$ |C| \le |B| \f$, and a list
 \f$ N \f$ holds at most one insertion or erasure and one change per
 attribute (plus an erasure and an insertion when the changes are missing),
 a constant; a hash table on the handles finds the list of each event in
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected time. \f$ \square \f$

 The global events cannot be moved past the events of the items, since an
 item inserted with an absolute key after a shift would be shifted twice;
 they bound the runs instead of going last. A relocation and a permutation
 change the meaning of the handles, and bound the runs for the same
 reason.

 A mutator tests two predicates before it builds an event of the kind
 \f$ E \f$: `listened< E >`, a constant of the subject, false for
 `NoObservers` and for static observers with no `on()` of \f$ E \f$, in which
 case the event, the read of the old state and the test vanish at compile
 time; and `live< E >()`, true when some observer wants \f$ E \f$ now (a
 constant for a static observer with no mask, a test of the bit of
 \f$ E \f$ in the union of the masks for the dynamic ones). The path that
 builds and sends the event is a cold function out of line when the subject
 is dynamic, so that the change with nobody attached costs the load and the
 test of the mask; `bench/zerocost.py` checks both claims by the
 instructions that callgrind counts and by the code of the changes, which
 with `NoObservers` is that of the plain structure byte for byte, and with a
 static observer that does not want the kind that of `NoObservers`.

 Outside a batch an insertion and a change are told after the change, an
 erasure before it; `on()` of the erasures and of the global events that
 lose information must not throw. When an `on()` of an insertion or a change
 throws, the observers already told get the inverse event in the reverse
 order, the structure undoes its own change and the exception goes on; this
 is the strong guarantee of Abrahams whenever the undo of the structure does
 not throw, and `prepare()` gives it with no undo, since it refuses before
 anything changes. A batch tells nothing before its close, so that a batch
 left by an exception is discarded: the structure goes back to its
 checkpoint if it has one, and the observers get \f$ \mathrm{Rebuilt} \f$
 otherwise. `UndoLog` keeps the events of a history, from which the inverses
 in the reverse order give a history back to any of its checkpoints.

 \section theory_ADS_capabilities Capabilities

 An algorithm that takes a structure as a type may ask what the structure
 can do, and choose its faster path at compile time; `Capabilities.h`
 gives the vocabulary of these questions, which is of three kinds because
 the answers are of three kinds. A concept of C++20 decides a question of
 syntax (whether `split()` exists and what it takes), but not one of
 meaning: a structure whose `rank()` scans its items satisfies the same
 concept as one whose `rank()` reads a counter, and the items of a heap
 that moves them satisfy the same requirements as those of a heap of
 nodes. The second kind is therefore an explicit opt-in of the structure,
 a variable template that it specializes (`enable_stable_references< S >`,
 as `std::ranges::enable_borrowed_range` of the standard) or a static
 member that it declares (`Q::decrease_key`, `S::monotone`); a structure
 that says nothing gets the conservative answer. The third kind is a
 property that a wrapper must pass on without knowing it, since a wrapper
 written today cannot list the properties of tomorrow: a query
 `s.query( tag )`, answered by the structure if it has a static member for
 the tag and by the default of the tag otherwise, which `ObservedGraph`
 forwards to its graph and `Shadow` composes from its two observers by the
 meet of the tag (the conjunction for the stable references and for the
 reads that may run with writes, the worse of the two classes of time for
 `rank()`), as the environments of P2300 forward their queries.

 The customization point objects of Niebler are used only where a type
 that ADS does not own may lack an operation that has a sensible default:
 `reserve( s , n )` does nothing for a `std::list`, `prefetch( s , k )`
 does nothing where no prefetch is known, and `insert_bulk( s , r )`
 inserts the items one by one; each calls the member if there is one, else
 a free function found by argument-dependent lookup, else the default. The
 three of the priority queues in `PriorityQueue.h` read the members of the
 book first and the spelling of the standard last, so that
 `std::priority_queue` models the base `PriorityQueue` as it is.

 A priority queue lowers the key of an item in one of two ways, and
 `decrease_key_of< Q >()` tells them apart: natively, by `decrease()` on
 the handle that the insertion gave, or lazily, by a new insertion of the
 item with the lower key, the older copies left in the queue and skipped
 when they come out. Neither is better in general: the lazy form needs no
 handles and no table of positions, at the price of a queue that holds one
 copy per decrease instead of one per item, and on sparse graphs it is
 often the faster of the two (Chen et al.). The shortest paths of
 `ShortestPaths.h` and Prim's algorithm of `MinimumSpanningTree.h` take
 either; in the lazy case the frontier keeps, for each node, whether it is
 in \f$ S \f$, and skips a copy whose node is not.

 <b>Lemma 2.</b> <em>Let the priorities of the copies be the pairs
 \f$ ( d , v ) \f$ of a distance and a node, inserted at each fall of the
 distance of \f$ v \f$. Then the lazy frontier extracts the same nodes, in
 the same order and with the same counts, as a native one on the same
 graph.</em>

 <em>Proof.</em> The distance of a node only falls, so that the last copy
 of \f$ v \f$ inserted has the least priority among the copies of
 \f$ v \f$ in the queue; while \f$ v \f$ is in \f$ S \f$, that copy is the
 one with the priority \f$ ( d_v , v ) \f$ which the native queue holds.
 When \f$ v \f$ leaves \f$ S \f$ its other copies have larger priorities and
 are skipped, and if \f$ v \f$ enters \f$ S \f$ again (with negative weights)
 its new copy is again the least of its copies. Hence at each extraction
 the least copy of a node in \f$ S \f$ is the least entry of the native
 queue, the pairs being distinct, and the stale copies before it are
 skipped without counting; the additions and the decreases are the calls
 of the prototype, the same in both. \f$ \square \f$

 \section theory_ADS_algebra The algebra of the aggregates

 A structure that keeps an aggregate, i.e., the combination
 \f$ a_1 \oplus \cdots \oplus a_n \f$ of some values by an associative
 operation with an identity \f$ e \f$ (the prefix sums of an array, the
 aggregate of a window, the augmentation of a tree), must update it when a
 value enters, leaves or changes, and the cheapest rule depends on the
 algebra of \f$ \oplus \f$, not on the structure. `Algebra.h` names the
 algebras by concepts on static members of the type `G` that defines
 \f$ \oplus \f$: a `Monoid` has `identity()` and `combine()`; a
 `CommutativeMonoid` declares `commutative`; a `Group` has `inverse()`; a
 `Selective` monoid declares `selective`, the promise that
 \f$ a \oplus b \in \{ a , b \} \f$; an `Action` of maps on a monoid has the
 identity map, the composition and the application of a map to the
 aggregate of \f$ n \f$ values, the ingredients of the lazy updates of a
 range. The flags are opt-ins, since no syntax shows that an operation
 commutes or selects, and the existing augmentations of `JoinTree`, which
 have `identity()`, `from()` and `combine()`, are monoids as they are. The
 concepts do not prove the laws, which the tests check on the algebras of
 ADS (the sums, the minima, the maxima) and on an operation that does not
 commute for each concept that does not require it.

 <b>Lemma 3.</b> <em>Let \f$ c = x \oplus a \oplus y \f$ be an aggregate in
 which the value \f$ a \f$ is replaced by \f$ b \f$, and
 \f$ c' = x \oplus b \oplus y \f$. (i) In an abelian group,
 \f$ c' = c \oplus a^{-1} \oplus b \f$. (ii) In a commutative monoid, if
 \f$ b = a \oplus d \f$ then \f$ c' = c \oplus d \f$. (iii) In a group, if
 \f$ x = e \f$ then \f$ c' = b \oplus a^{-1} \oplus c \f$; if \f$ y = e \f$,
 \f$ c' = c \oplus a^{-1} \oplus b \f$. (iv) In a selective monoid, the
 aggregate of any values is one of them, or \f$ e \f$ if there is
 none.</em>

 <em>Proof.</em> (i) By commutativity \f$ c = a \oplus x \oplus y \f$, so
 that \f$ c \oplus a^{-1} \oplus b = a \oplus a^{-1} \oplus x \oplus y
 \oplus b = x \oplus b \oplus y \f$. (ii) \f$ c \oplus d = x \oplus y \oplus
 a \oplus d = x \oplus ( a \oplus d ) \oplus y \f$. (iii) With
 \f$ x = e \f$, \f$ b \oplus a^{-1} \oplus c = b \oplus a^{-1} \oplus a
 \oplus y = b \oplus y \f$, and symmetrically with \f$ y = e \f$, by
 associativity alone. (iv) By induction on the number of operands, each
 combination being one of its two operands. \f$ \square \f$

 Lemma 3 is the dispatch of the structures: (i) is the update of a prefix
 sum by its difference (`Fenwick`, see \ref theory_sequence_fenwick) and
 the free partial retroactivity of an abelian group, (ii) the `add()` of a
 commutative monoid (a maximum raised), (iii) the removal of the first
 value of a window and the insertion of the last (subtract-on-evict, see
 \ref theory_queue_sliding), which needs no commutativity, and (iv) the
 monotone deque of a window of minima. A monoid with none of these
 properties can only recombine the parts that the change touched, which
 the structures do in \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ combinations (a tree) or in
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized (two stacks). When the values are not exact
 (`exact_v` false, i.e., floating point) the inverse of (i) and (iii)
 does not undo the rounding of the combination, and a group aggregate
 drifts; the structures then recompute it from the values every
 `period()` updates, at \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized cost when the period is at
 least the number of values, and their audits catch a drift beyond the
 tolerance of a test.

